{"context":"eka-steward must read/write EKA knowledge and orchestrate external agent CLIs without becoming a second source of truth. eka-core's `runtime/` and `store/` packages are explicitly the INTERNAL API (ADR-014: no interface types, 'introduced when a consumer actually requires them'); eka-core has no tag-driven release workflow today (req:release-coordination), and the release-cadence lock already bit once (eka-mcp pinned core v1.13.0 vs eka-cli v1.14.0, fixed by ADR-030). Steward is the consumer that triggers ADR-014's deferred interface requirement.","decision":"Steward imports a versioned PUBLIC contract package (`eka-core/api`) — interfaces plus a documented compatibility/deprecation window — never `runtime/` or `store/`. M6 ships WI `core-public-contract` before `release-2-0`; ADR-030 is the precedent. The eka CLI subprocess is an escape hatch behind one adapter with a documented allow-list. MCP is rejected as the steward-EKA integration ONLY (workers MAY use MCP as a capability). Steward is the SOLE EKA store writer; the invariant is ENFORCED by mechanism (separate UID / FS confinement sealing `~/.eka`, and `eka` absent from worker PATH), not by convention. Separate repo, version 2.0.0, own tag train. All EKA writes must state their ADR-032 provenance/adopt semantics (repo-context publish, or workspace-native + explicit adopt).","consequences":"A durable, semver'd boundary decouples steward from eka-core internals; the escape hatch isolates subprocess coupling. Cost: M6 must define and freeze `api`; steward tracks `api` semver; no consumer may import `runtime/`/`store/` after v2.","alternativesConsidered":"Import `runtime/` directly (rejected: ADR-014 internal, unversioned). MCP-only (rejected: hop, non-determinism, trust surface, and wrong layer). CLI-only (rejected: heavy per-call, weaker typing). Embed core in cli (rejected: couples release trains)."}